ghosts:
  - id: "GHOST-001"
    tier: "warm"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-23"
    titre: "Archive before dashboard"
    l0_abstract: "Implement `scribe archive` before `scribe dashboard` so memory lifecycle rules exist before visualization."
    l2_details: >
      Dashboard is useful for observability, but it should consume stable lifecycle semantics rather than invent them.
      Archive is the lower-level primitive: it can move cold entries out of the hot operational path, preserve causality,
      and keep future dashboard views smaller, faster, and less misleading.
    alternatives_rejetees:
      - nom: "scribe dashboard first"
        raison_rejet: "A dashboard before archive would visualize a growing YAML without solving retention, cold storage, or signal/noise pressure."
    evidence:
      type: "REASONED"
      source: "JOURNAL-025"
      observable: "`compact/promote/export` are validated; the next missing lifecycle primitive is archival, not presentation."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-025"
      renforce: ["PAT-007", "PAT-008", "PAT-009"]
  - id: "GHOST-002"
    tier: "hot"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    date: "2026-05-24"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-24"
    titre: "Lock multi-agent local choisi"
    l0_abstract: "SCRIBE mutations use one local JSON lock in `scribe-out/locks/scribe.lock` before adding database or server coordination."
    l2_details: >
      The immediate risk is concurrent local agent writes to the same YAML memory. A tiny lock file with agent, session,
      pid, timestamp, surface, and TTL prevents accidental overlap while staying portable with the rootless bundle.
      Doctor and read-only commands stay unblocked so diagnosis remains possible during lock ownership.
    alternatives_rejetees:
      - nom: "Pas de lock"
        raison_rejet: "Corruption possible si deux agents modifient le SCRIBE en parallèle."
      - nom: "Lock base de données"
        raison_rejet: "Sur-ingénierie pour un bundle local portable et copiable entre projets."
    ne_pas_reproposer: ["Pas de lock", "Lock base de données"]
    evidence:
      type: "OBSERVED"
      source: "JOURNAL-045"
      observable: "`scribe lock acquire/status/release` fonctionne et les commandes mutantes refusent sans verrou actif."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-045"
      renforce: ["SCAR-001", "PAT-009", "PAT-020"]
  - id: "GHOST-003"
    tier: "hot"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    date: "2026-05-24"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-24"
    titre: "state.json partagé pour resynchronisation multi-agent"
    l0_abstract: "A shared `scribe-out/state.json` records the last real SCRIBE writer and hash so agents can detect stale context before acting."
    l2_details: >
      The lock prevents concurrent writes, but it does not tell a second agent that its context is stale after another agent has already written.
      The chosen state file stores a hash, line count, writer object, last session, changed IDs, and closed write_kind enum. `sync` reads and alerts
      without becoming the last writer; `sync --repair` atomically rebuilds state after legitimate manual recovery.
    alternatives_rejetees:
      - nom: "HEAD.json"
        raison_rejet: "The Git analogy implies branch/version semantics the SCRIBE state file does not provide."
      - nom: "sync met à jour last_writer"
        raison_rejet: "A reader is not an écrivain; overwriting last_writer hides the true source of the latest memory mutation."
      - nom: "last_entry_id seul"
        raison_rejet: "Tier/status/archive mutations can change SCRIBE state without appending a new entity."
    ne_pas_reproposer: ["HEAD.json", "sync met à jour last_writer", "last_entry_id seul"]
    evidence:
      type: "OBSERVED"
      source: "JOURNAL-046"
      observable: "`scribe sync` reports stale/missing state, `sync --repair` writes state.json atomically, and doctor warns W011/W012/W013."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-046"
      renforce: ["GHOST-002", "PAT-020", "PAT-003"]
  - id: "GHOST-004"
    tier: "warm"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    date: "2026-05-25"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-25"
    titre: "Migration Clean Architecture progressive du frontend"
    l0_abstract: "The JJK frontend now migrates through thin App Router routes, core-layer source of truth, and legacy re-export adapters instead of a big-bang path rewrite."
    l2_details: >
      The clean architecture pack was applied as a progressive migration: `src/app` delegates to presentation components,
      business validation/use cases live under `core/domain` and `core/application`, and Redux/RTK Query/realtime details
      live under `core/infrastructure`. Legacy paths remain as re-export adapters to preserve hidden imports while new code
      imports from `src/core/*` or `src/shared/*`.
    alternatives_rejetees:
      - nom: "Big-bang deletion of old frontend paths"
        raison_rejet: "Too risky in a dirty worktree and unnecessary because compatibility re-exports preserve runtime behavior during migration."
      - nom: "Introduce Next proxy during this refactor"
        raison_rejet: "The backend contract currently exposes direct REST plus WebSocket send; adding a proxy would be a behavior and deployment change, not only a clean-architecture restructure."
    ne_pas_reproposer: ["Big-bang deletion of old frontend paths", "Silent Next proxy switch"]
    evidence:
      type: "OBSERVED"
      source: "JOURNAL-057"
      observable: "`npm run lint`, `npx tsc --noEmit`, `npm run build`, `curl -I /login`, Chromium headless render, and `graphify update .` all passed after the migration."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-057"
      renforce: ["PAT-024", "INV-F003"]
  - id: "GHOST-005"
    tier: "warm"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    date: "2026-05-25"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-25"
    titre: "Cookie-backed auth boundary choisi"
    l0_abstract: "Auth now treats browser JavaScript as untrusted: access and refresh secrets live in HttpOnly cookies, while the frontend only stores session identity."
    l2_details: >
      The hardening repays the browser-stored JWT debt without inserting a Next.js proxy layer. Direct backend cookies keep the existing REST and Socket.IO topology, refresh rotation narrows stolen-refresh-token lifetime, and a readable CSRF cookie plus origin policy protects unsafe browser requests without exposing bearer tokens to Redux or localStorage.
    alternatives_rejetees:
      - nom: "Keep JWT in localStorage for simplicity"
        raison_rejet: "A single XSS would expose the bearer token and keep the original DEBT-003 production gap alive."
      - nom: "Move auth through a Next.js proxy in this step"
        raison_rejet: "The current deployment already has direct REST plus Socket.IO; adding a proxy would widen the behavior and ops change beyond the debt repayment."
    ne_pas_reproposer: ["Browser-stored bearer JWT", "Silent Next proxy switch during auth debt repayment"]
    evidence:
      type: "OBSERVED"
      source: "JOURNAL-058"
      observable: "Backend and frontend builds pass after HttpOnly cookie sessions, refresh rotation, CSRF header validation, origin checks, and removal of Redux/localStorage token persistence."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-058"
      rembourse: ["DEBT-003"]
      renforce: ["GHOST-004", "VAC-003", "INV-F003"]
hypotheses: []
lessons: []
debts:
  - id: "DEBT-001"
    tier: "warm"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    titre: "Process-local auth rate limiter"
    date_creation: "2026-05-23"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-23"
    type: "CHOISIE"
    severite: "HIGH"
    l0_abstract: "The auth limiter is intentionally process-local; replace it with shared enforcement before horizontal scaling."
    l2_details: >
      This session chose an in-memory limiter to harden the current single-process app without adding infrastructure.
      In multi-instance production, each process would keep separate counters, so enforcement must move to Redis,
      an API gateway, or a WAF before horizontal scaling.
    plan_remboursement: "Before multi-instance production, replace the in-memory store with Redis/gateway/WAF rate limiting and configure proxy trust deliberately."
  - id: "DEBT-002"
    tier: "cold"
    status: "REMBOURSEE"
    titre: "Smoke retrieval graphify dual ambiguity"
    date_creation: "2026-05-24"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-24"
    scope: "dev"
    type: "OBSERVED_RETRIEVAL_GAP"
    severite: "MEDIUM"
    l0_abstract: "`scribe eval --smoke` originally missed the graphify application/bundle confusion owner; PAT-024 now owns that distinction."
    evidence:
      type: "OBSERVED"
      source: "JOURNAL-050"
      observable: "`scribe eval --smoke` passed 4/4 after the smoke expectation moved from PAT-023 to dedicated PAT-024 and the bundle graph output path was renamed to `scribe-out/bundle-graph/`."
    l2_details: >
      The failure was a retrieval-quality gap, not a runtime crash. PAT-023 remains the cleanup/noise rule;
      PAT-024 now owns the distinct Graphify app-vs-bundle graph naming rule.
    plan_remboursement: "REMBOURSEE: PAT-024 hot plus `scribe-out/bundle-graph/` rename resolved the semantic owner and `scribe eval --smoke` reaches 4/4."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-049"
      contribue_a: ["PAT-024"]
    status_log:
      - date: "2026-05-24"
        from: "ACTIVE"
        to: "REMBOURSEE"
        reason: "PAT-024 and the bundle-graph rename gave the smoke query a dedicated causal owner."
    tier_log:
      - date: "2026-05-24"
        from: "warm"
        to: "cold"
        reason: "Debt is repaid and kept searchable as historical retrieval governance."
  - id: "DEBT-003"
    tier: "cold"
    status: "REMBOURSEE"
    titre: "Browser-stored JWT session"
    date_creation: "2026-05-25"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-25"
    type: "CHOISIE"
    severite: "HIGH"
    l0_abstract: "The frontend no longer persists bearer JWTs in JavaScript-readable storage; session secrets are backend-set cookies with refresh rotation and CSRF/origin checks."
    l2_details: >
      DEBT-003 is repaid by moving access and refresh credentials to HttpOnly backend cookies, rotating refresh tokens with family reuse revocation, storing only public session identity in Redux, cleaning legacy localStorage keys, and requiring CSRF plus origin validation for unsafe browser requests.
    plan_remboursement: "REMBOURSEE: backend cookie/refresh-token support, frontend cookie-backed session hydration, and removal of token reads from Redux-visible client state are implemented and validated in JOURNAL-058."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-058"
      explique_par: ["GHOST-005"]
      renforce: ["VAC-003", "INV-F003"]
    status_log:
      - date: "2026-05-25"
        from: "ACTIVE"
        to: "REMBOURSEE"
        reason: "HttpOnly cookie sessions, refresh rotation, CSRF/origin policy, and frontend token removal now replace browser-stored JWTs."
    tier_log:
      - date: "2026-05-25"
        from: "warm"
        to: "cold"
        reason: "Debt is repaid and retained as historical auth governance."
mutations: []
journal:
  - id: "JOURNAL-001"
    date: "2026-05-20"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    l0_abstract: "Initialized TENOR session memory after confirming the project SCRIBE and template were absent."
    pourquoi: "The user requested TENOR initialization and last_update synchronization; creating a minimal YAML memory prevents future sessions from starting without causal memory."
  - id: "JOURNAL-002"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    l0_abstract: "Updated framework dependencies to current stable versions and repaired the lint/build path exposed by Next.js 16."
    pourquoi: "The dependency upgrade was not just a version bump: Next.js 16 removes `next lint`, so keeping the old lint command would make the project look upgraded while leaving validation broken."
  - id: "JOURNAL-003"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    l0_abstract: "Installed Graphify for Codex and generated the canonical root knowledge graph."
    pourquoi: "The project needed persistent structural memory aligned with Codex hooks; keeping the graph at root prevents future agents from missing it or reading a stale subfolder graph."
  - id: "JOURNAL-004"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    l0_abstract: "Implemented a read-only scribe doctor CLI with a Markdown report output."
    pourquoi: "The SCRIBE now needs mechanical validation before V3.2 migration work; keeping doctor read-only prevents tooling from silently rewriting historical memory."
  - id: "JOURNAL-005"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    l0_abstract: "Validated the existing SCRIBE with doctor: zero blocking ERRORs, five legacy WARNINGs intentionally kept."
    pourquoi: "The V3.2 roadmap forbids retroactive cleanup of old warnings during migration; documenting W001/W002/W009 preserves historical integrity while proving the SCRIBE is structurally safe."
    migration_warnings:
      errors_blocking: 0
      warnings_total: 5
      kept_as_legacy:
        - "W001: PAT-001 and VAC-001 pre-V3.2 missing scope."
        - "W002: PAT-001 and VAC-001 pre-V3.2 missing evidence.type."
        - "W009: legacy entries pre-V3.2 missing schema_patch_date."
  - id: "JOURNAL-006"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "QUICK"
    l0_abstract: "Engraved the full seven-step operational loop into the V3.2 scribe workflow template."
    pourquoi: "The prior wording could make agents treat scribe doctor as the last memory step; the template now states explicitly that every real task must write the causal SCRIBE delta before the final doctor check."
  - id: "JOURNAL-007"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    hot_entries_consulted: ["VAC-001", "PAT-001"]
    l0_abstract: "Hardened Socket.IO sendMessage ingestion with server-side validation and PII-safe logging."
    pourquoi: "WebSocket events bypass Express/Zod middleware, so the same production input-trust risk exists unless the socket boundary validates unknown payloads itself; logging only metadata prevents chat content exposure in debug logs."
    scribe_delta: "VAC-002"
  - id: "JOURNAL-008"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    hot_entries_consulted: ["PAT-001", "VAC-002"]
    l0_abstract: "Separated Graphify app scope from SCRIBE/TENOR tooling with .graphifyignore and an APP_SCOPE query convention."
    pourquoi: "The canonical root graph must guide application architecture; after tooling nodes polluted god-node rankings, excluding process tooling restored app-first navigation without deleting the tooling itself."
    scribe_delta: "PAT-002"
  - id: "JOURNAL-009"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    hot_entries_consulted: ["VAC-002", "PAT-002"]
    l0_abstract: "Added request throttling to public auth attempts before validation and controller execution."
    pourquoi: "Login and registration are public abuse targets; limiting attempts at the route boundary reduces brute-force pressure while keeping validation and business logic unchanged."
    scribe_delta: "VAC-003 | DEBT-001"
  - id: "JOURNAL-010"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    hot_entries_consulted: ["PAT-002", "VAC-003"]
    l0_abstract: "Validated scribe doctor with a controlled corruption crash-test on an isolated copy."
    pourquoi: "The memory validator must fail closed before it can be trusted; injecting duplicate IDs, missing V3.2 fields, broken links, and an unlogged status mutation proved doctor catches structural corruption while the canonical SCRIBE remains clean."
    scribe_delta: "JOURNAL-010"
  - id: "JOURNAL-011"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    hot_entries_consulted: ["PAT-002", "VAC-003", "PAT-003"]
    l0_abstract: "Promoted scribe doctor into a mandatory pre/post guard for every SCRIBE evolution."
    pourquoi: "After the controlled corruption test, doctor is no longer optional process hygiene; it is the mechanical gate that proves a memory delta starts from a sane state and does not corrupt the canonical SCRIBE."
    scribe_delta: "PAT-003 | JOURNAL-011"
  - id: "JOURNAL-012"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    hot_entries_consulted: ["PAT-003", "PAT-002"]
    l0_abstract: "Added a reusable scribe guard command and future-agent instructions for pre/post doctor enforcement."
    pourquoi: "A rule inside the workflow can still be forgotten by a future agent; exposing `./scribe guard -- <command>` and documenting it in AGENTS.md turns the doctor guard into a repeatable mechanism for command-based SCRIBE mutations."
    scribe_delta: "JOURNAL-012"
  - id: "JOURNAL-013"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    hot_entries_consulted: ["PAT-003", "PAT-002"]
    l0_abstract: "Created a separate SCRIBE-RAG engineering workspace under the workflow directory."
    pourquoi: "Future query/challenge/RAG design needs a place for specs and experiments without contaminating the active SCRIBE protocol or the canonical project memory."
